cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "originshift: US non-preferential rules of origin, compiled from the eCFR"
message: >-
  If you use this corpus or the determinations it produces, please cite it. The
  concept DOI below always resolves to the latest version; cite the version DOI
  when you need to pin what you answered under.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kvasha
    given-names: Ihor
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-4814-0827"
    affiliation: Independent
repository-code: "https://github.com/KvashaIhor/originshift"
url: "https://pypi.org/project/originshift/"
abstract: >-
  Compiles 19 CFR 102.20 and 102.21 out of the eCFR into a machine-readable
  corpus, answers which country a good is legally from, and cites the rule it
  used. Every record carries the source URL, the eCFR issue date and the
  nomenclature vintage it answers under, so a rule can be re-derived after the
  source is amended. Defects found in the source text are reported rather than
  corrected. Scored against CBP's own HQ rulings, with the denominators, the
  exclusions and every disagreement published.
keywords:
  - rules of origin
  - non-preferential origin
  - customs
  - trade compliance
  - 19 CFR 102
  - country of origin
  - textiles and apparel
  - HTSUS
  - USMCA
  - rules as code
